This directory contains a script to help compiling Geneva, plus a number of
helper scripts for running and testing Geneva jobs (locally and across hosts).

Build
-----

prepareBuild.sh
   A wrapper around CMake that eases compilation. Reads its settings from a
   .gcfg file (defaulting to genevaConfig.gcfg).
   Usage: ./prepareBuild.sh [<config.gcfg>] [--clean] [--build] [-y|--yes] [--help]
   Typical: cp genevaConfig.gcfg myConfig.gcfg && edit it, then
            (cd ~/build && /path/to/scripts/prepareBuild.sh myConfig.gcfg --clean -y && make -j)

genevaConfig.gcfg
   The configuration file for prepareBuild.sh (Boost location, build type,
   examples/tests/benchmarks, CUDA/MPI, etc.).

Running networked jobs (server + clients)
-----------------------------------------

A networked Geneva program is started once as a server and any number of times
as a client (--client). Both helper scripts take an optional consumer mnemonic:
"asio" (default) or "beast" (websocket), and map it to the correct command-line
options (--asio_ip/--asio_port resp. --beast_ip/--beast_port).

startLocalJobs.sh
   Starts a Geneva program on the LOCAL host (reliable, low-latency networking)
   -- for testing networked execution without a cluster. Cleans up all child
   processes on exit.
   Usage: ./startLocalJobs.sh <program> <n_clients> <port> [consumer: asio|beast|mpi]
   For asio/beast it runs the program once as a server plus n_clients times as
   clients over local TCP. For mpi it launches the program ONCE via mpirun with
   n_clients + 1 ranks (one rank-0 master plus n_clients workers); the port is
   ignored (MPI uses no listening socket -- pass any placeholder, e.g. 0) and the
   launcher can be overridden with the MPIRUN environment variable.
   Set the environment variable GENEVA_RUN_TIMEOUT=<seconds> to run
   non-interactively: it then waits up to that long for the server (or mpirun job)
   to finish, tears everything down, and returns its exit code (124 on timeout).
   Without it, the server/mpirun output is tailed until interrupted.

startClients.sh
   Starts a Geneva program N times as clients only (no server) -- they connect
   to an already-running server (e.g. started by startLocalJobs.sh or on another
   host). Cleans up the started clients on interrupt.
   Usage: ./startClients.sh <program> <n_clients> <ip/hostname> <port> [consumer: asio|beast]

Driving many hosts (cluster helpers)
------------------------------------

sshList.sh
   Runs a command on every host listed in a host file (one host per line; the
   file extension MUST be .hcfg), via ssh.
   Usage: ./sshList.sh <optional ssh options> <hostFile.hcfg> <command>

rsyncList.sh
   rsyncs a local directory to a target directory on every host in a host file.
   Usage: ./rsyncList.sh <options> </local/dir> <hostFile.hcfg:/remote/dir> <options>

sampleHostFile.hcfg
   A sample host file (one host per line) for sshList.sh and rsyncList.sh.

Configuration & testing helpers
-------------------------------

updateConfigFiles.sh
   Regenerates/updates the JSON configuration files of all tests and examples in
   the source tree from a fully built binary tree. (Running any Geneva binary in
   a directory that contains an empty ./config folder auto-creates its default
   configs; this script just automates that across the whole tree and copies the
   results back into the source tree.)
   Usage: ./updateConfigFiles.sh <GENEVA_SOURCE_DIR> <GENEVA_BUILD_DIR>

Testing/repeatedRun.sh
   Runs a given command N times and appends each run's output (with a timestamp)
   to a result file -- useful for shaking out intermittent/flaky behaviour.
   Usage: ./Testing/repeatedRun.sh <command> <count> <result_file>
